 being  desensitized to this because it's so  common gun violence in America how do  you do that  you know it's such a great question and  and I look back  and I'll just use Sandy Hook as an  example  I felt horrible emotion over it anger  over it I watched all the news reporting  over it and I did nothing  I did nothing  um  and I and I lived in this bubble I mean  this was Parkland Florida this was the  community actually a lot like Newtown  where you never see this happening  um and  I allowed myself to stay in this bubble  part of what I do now to help people  care  people always say to me I can't imagine  how you feel  I go out of my way every chance I get  to help people imagine how I feel  to help people imagine the terror to  help people imagine the violence and to  help people imagine themselves in that  and not me because I think at the end of  the day  the more people realize while this  happened to somebody else  but it could happen to me when I go to  the mall it could happen to me when I go  to the shopping center or the movie it  could happen to my family when I send  them to school  the more likely they are to become part  of solving it and I will tell you if you  look at the last three elections in this  country  doing something about gun violence has  been a top voting issue it has changed  election outcomes and I think people are  listening now and I if you if you look  at polls  over 80 percent of America wants to  solve this we are winning the fight  but moving the political system in this  country is a lot of work well I mean you  say that as well but I'm also aware that  we've seen recently in states where  there have been mass shootings  politicians re-elected who are anti-gun  reform as well I mean there is that  reality they may have been voted for for  other policies politics is not a one  policy thing of course but you've spoken  very publicly about your bond with the  president with Joe Biden yeah I know  that he was an important figure 